SOAPBOX: I wish this contest didn't fall on weekends where exciting Formula 1 races
SOAPBOX: take place. So I missed time for that, but honestly, both were very exciting.
SOAPBOX: I decided from the beginning not to use the Flex 8400 (which I've decided
SOAPBOX: to sell.) As a result I stayed with the IC-7610 and had no regrets. There were
SOAPBOX: times I hit over 60 QSOs per hour. I think for the forseeable future, I'm
SOAPBOX: sticking with what I know. I did order the new IC-7300 Mk2 and it will be
SOAPBOX: interesting to see how it's receiver stacks up to the 7610.
SOAPBOX: I was happy to actually make 2 160 contacts! I think one of my projects if the
SOAPBOX: weather improves is to put my 160 inverted L. Also the 80M vertical served a
SOAPBOX: purpose and I had more contacts on it than I expected. It's a good antenna
SOAPBOX: for transmitting. I may add a trap for 40M as having a full sized 40M vertical
SOAPBOX: could come in handy. At any rate, I will be adding more counterpoise(s? |
SOAPBOX: radials?) to it.
SOAPBOX: Now that I've gotten over my shyness about programming in C++ again,
SOAPBOX: I've started work on a dedicated Morse code decoder and my station
SOAPBOX: controller. I'm finding some limitations in the Icom CIV commands (the
SOAPBOX: most aggregious is no command to auto-tune CW and RTTY) but I will possibly be
SOAPBOX: able to work around it.
SOAPBOX: One thing I did notice was that the N1MM+ I/Q display seems to be more sensitive
SOAPBOX: to receiving weak signals than I can hear. I don't use CW Skimmer and
SOAPBOX: I'm not sure if the sensitivity would carry over to it, but I noticed N1MM+
SOAPBOX: identified signals (and displayed them) that I could not hear. Perhaps my
SOAPBOX: "low noise" filter setup is killing them off. Only time (and
SOAPBOX: programming) will tell for sure.
